            <ArticleTitle>Entrapment of the flexor hallucis longus tendon following ankle arthrodesis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>45-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.foot.2016.01.001</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0958-2592(16)00002-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Impingement following arthroscopic ankle arthrodesis has not been reported in the literature previously. We present a case report of a 68-year-old male 9 months following an uncomplicated arthroscopic ankle fusion presenting with persistent posteromedial ankle pain. Flexor hallucis longus (FHL) tendon impingement resulting from a prominent os trigonum was identified. This was successfully treated utilising hindfoot endoscopy with excision of the os trigonum and FHL release.</AbstractText>
